"CIDR Proceedings (https://www.vldb.org/cidrdb/2025/linear-elastic-caching-via-ski-rental.html)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Download PDF ... In this work we study the Linear Elastic Caching problem, where the goal is to minimize the total cost of a cache inclusive of not just its misses, but also its memory footprint integrated over time.\n\n#### Linear Elastic Caching via Ski Rental\n\nAuthors:\n\nRavi Kumar, Todd Lipcon, Manish Purohit, Tamas Sarlos\n\nDownload PDF\n\n##### Abstract\n\nIn this work we study the Linear Elastic Caching problem, where the goal is to minimize the total cost of a cache inclusive of not just its misses, but also its memory footprint integrated over time. We demonstrate a theoretical connection to the classic ski rental problem and propose a practical algorithm that combines online caching algorithms with ski rental policies. We also introduce a lightweight machine learning-based algorithm for ski rental that is optimized for production workloads and is easy to integrate within existing database systems. Evaluations on both production workloads in Google Spanner and publicly available traces show that the proposed elastic caching approach can significantly reduce the total cache cost compared to traditional fixed-size cache policies.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLinear Elastic Caching via Ski Rental (https://vldb.org/cidrdb/papers/2025/p22-kumar.pdf)\nciteturn9search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; CIDR’25, January 19-22, 2025, Amsterdam, The Netherlands ... In all subsequent results, algorithms labeled as “Breakeven” implement Algorithm 1 using Breakeven as the Ski Rental algorithm and GDSF as the eviction policy, and similarly for the three other elastic caching algorithms. ... * We note that the cost of a cache eviction relative to the cost of RAM-seconds is an important factor that determines the efficacy of linear elastic caching algorithms.\nCIDR’25, January 19-22, 2025, Amsterdam, The Netherlands  \nRavi Kumar, Todd Lipcon, Manish Purohit, and Tamas Sarlos\n\nset the TTL to be precomputed best TTL for that page. Otherwise, we set the TTL using either the breakeven or randomized policies.\n\nSince different pages occupy different sizes in the cache, we mainly utilize the *Greedy Dual Size Frequency (GDSF)* [7] algorithm as the cache eviction policy. We note that we replicated the results using the new S3FIFO [28] eviction policy and the results remain qualitatively unchanged. In all subsequent results, algorithms labeled as “Breakeven” implement Algorithm 1 using Breakeven as the Ski Rental algorithm and GDSF as the eviction policy, and similarly for the three other elastic caching algorithms. The algorithm labeled “GDSF” is the baseline that uses a fixed cache size and has no ski rental component.\n\n*Costs.* We note that the cost of a cache eviction relative to the cost of RAM-seconds is an important factor that determines the efficacy of linear elastic caching algorithms. \n*Effect on Miss Rate.* We have assumed for the purposes of computing TCO that the cost of cache misses (or evictions) can be\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nLine chart titled by caption Figure 3: Variation of miss rate with cache size.  \nAxes: y-axis “Miss rate”; x-axis “Cache Size (MiB)”.  \nLegend: GDSF (pink/red line with circles), Breakeven (green down triangles), Randomized (blue up triangles), Learned + Breakeven (purple left triangles), Learned + Randomized (orange right triangles).  \nGDSF miss rate decreases from about 0.44 to near 0.01 as cache size increases; ski-rental markers appear at larger cache sizes with low miss rates.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 3: Variation of miss rate with cache size.\n\n*Sensitivity to the Cost Ratio.* A dominant factor underlying the efficacy of elastic caching policies is the ratio of the cost of a cache eviction to the cost of RAM (per byte-second). Figure 4 shows a plot of TCO improvement for a wide range of different cost ratios for the Tencent Block trace. As the cost of evictions increases, the relative cost of maintaining the cache in RAM decreases and the gains due to elastic caching reduce.\n\n8  CONCLUSIONS\n\nWe introduced and studied the linear elastic caching problem, motivated by reducing both the cache miss cost and the cache maintenance cost. Through an interesting connection to the ski rental problem, we developed simple and practical algorithms for linear elastic caching. These algorithms reduce the total cost of ownership on publicly available cache traces. We also propose a lightweight--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCIDR Proceedings (https://vldb.org/cidrdb/2025/)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; #### Proceedings of CIDR ...  PDF ... ##### Session 5: BETTER DATA PROCESSING ... Linear Elastic Caching via Ski Rental\n\nHolger Pirk, David Conor Loughlin\n\n PDF\n\nOLTP Through the Looking Glass 16 Years Later: Communication is the New Bottleneck\n\nXinjing Zhou, Viktor Leis, Xiangyao Yu, Michael Stonebraker\n\n PDF\n\n##### Session 5: BETTER DATA PROCESSING\n\nRuntime-Extensible Parsers\n\nHannes Mühleisen, Mark Raasveldt\n\n PDF\n\nTowards Functional Decomposition of Storage Formats\n\nMartin Prammer, Xinyu Zeng, Ruijun Meng, Wes McKinney, Huanchen Zhang, Andrew Pavlo, Jignesh M Patel\n\n PDF\n\nAdaptive data transformations for QaaS\n\nDimitrios Koutsoukos, Renato Marroquín, Ingo Müller, Ana Klimovic\n\n PDF\n\nAdaptive Factorization Using Linear-Chained Hash Tables\n\nPaul Groß, Daniel ten Wolde, Peter Boncz\n\n PDF\n\n##### Session 6: CLOUD AND MORE\n\nLinear Elastic Caching via Ski Rental\n\nRavi Kumar, Todd Lipcon, Manish Purohit, Tamas Sarlos\n\n PDF\n\nVectraFlow: Integrating Vectors into Stream Processing\n\nDuo Lu, Siming Feng, Jonathan Zhou, Franco Solleza, Malte Schwarzkopf, Uğur Çetintemel\n---------------------------------------------------------