e metrics were gathered. \nMetrics: throughput, average latency for read and update operations, cache hit ratio \nFind maximum performance, Workload B \nSame as previous, but with workload B. \nFind relationship of latency on throughput, Workload A \nRun YCSB Workload A on the cluster while throttling throughput from YCSB.  The throughput \nwas increased until the it reaches the maximum found in the prior tests.  The throughput levels \ntested were:  1k, 2k, 4k, 6k, 8k, 10k, 15k, 20k, 25k, 30k, 35k, 40k, 45k, 50k, 75k, 100k, 125k, \n150k, 175k, 200k, 250k, 300k, 350k, 400k, 450k, but in some cases not every point was plotted \nto increase clarity. \nNumber of operations for each throughput level: 10,000,000 \nMetrics: graphs of average latency vs. throughput for read and update operations \nFind relationship of latency on throughput, Workload B \nSame as previous, but with workload B. \nMetrics: graphs of average latency vs. throughput for read and update operations \n© Copyright 2013, Thumbtack Technology, Inc. \n40 of 43--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLinear Elastic Caching via Ski Rental — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/linear-elastic-caching-via-ski-rental/wrk_50e6386b7a3d59bf12b6e0bf426fdea8)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Cidr 2025 ... Traditional cache management research focuses almost exclusively on maximizing hit rates within a fixed memory budget. ... This paper introduces Linear Elastic Caching, a framework that minimizes the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) by dynamically balancing the cost of cache misses against the cost of holding data in memory over time.\n\n# Linear Elastic Caching via Ski Rental\n\nRavi Kumar 0001, Todd Lipcon, Manish Purohit, Tamás Sarlós\n\nCidr 2025\n\n## Introduction\n\nTraditional cache management research focuses almost exclusively on maximizing hit rates within a fixed memory budget. However, in modern cloud environments, memory is a metered resource where keeping a page in RAM incurs a continuous \"rent\" cost. This paper introduces Linear Elastic Caching, a framework that minimizes the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) by dynamically balancing the cost of cache misses against the cost of holding data in memory over time.\n\n## The Ski Rental Framework\n\nThe core innovation of this work is modeling cache eviction as an instance of the classic Ski Rental Problem. In this analogy, keeping a page in RAM is like \"renting\" skis at a daily rate ($r$), while a cache miss is like \"buying\" skis at a fixed cost ($B$). If the time until the next access is unknown, the optimal strategy is to \"rent\" (keep the page) until the cumulative rent equals the purchase price, then \"buy\" (evict the page).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptimizing service migration in AC3: A cache-aided, data-mobility-aware framework - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1084804526001220)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 2 months ago; Content-centric caching with RL: Deep reinforcement learning (DRL) has been applied to content placement and migration in vehicular and edge networks (e.g., Malektaji et al. ... We formulate service migration as a joint optimization problem that explicitly incorporates latency, migration cost, mobility-induced distance variation, and cache occupancy. ... Traffic Transp. ... ### Joint optimization of preference-aware caching and content migration in cost-efficient mobile edge networks\n\n## Journal of Network and Computer Applications\n\nVolume 254, October 2026, 104547\n\n\nResearch Paper\n\nOptimizing service migration in AC3: A cache-aided, data-mobility-aware framework\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnca.2026.104547Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe service migration mechanism is crucial for achieving low-latency performance in mobile edge computing (MEC). However, most existing approaches focus on optimizing migration, caching, or resource allocation separately. In stateless cloud-native environments, migration performance is heavily influenced by data locality. After relocation, services need to re-fetch their state, which adds extra latency. This work presents a cache-aided, mobility-aware service migration framework. It integrates cache occupancy and user–server proximity into the state space of a reinforcement learning (RL) agent. Rather than proposing a new RL algorithm. We formulates service migration as a joint latency–migration cost optimization problem. While effective under stable mobility patterns, such approaches may incur high computational overhead and reduced robustness in heterogeneous environments. Other optimization-based methods (e.g., heuristic or cost-driven formulations) typically assume simplified mobility models or static conditions. In this context, RL offers an alternative data-driven approach that adapts decisions based on observed state transitions, without requiring explicit trajectory prediction.\n\nThe contributions of this work are primarily methodological and system-oriented, rather than algorithmic. The contributions are:\n\n  * •\n\nWe formulate service migration as a joint optimization problem that explicitly incorporates latency, migration cost, mobility-induced distance variation, and cache occupancy.\n\n  * •\n\nWe integrate cache state as a contextual signal within the RL state space to guide migration decisions for stateless cloud-native services, shifting cache-awareness from content optimization to service placement.\n\n  * •\n\nWe design and implement a unified simulation framework based on a 5G-compliant environment that models heterogeneous mobility patterns (pedestrian, bicycle, vehicle, IoT) and resource-diverse MEC nodes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA service cache-based dynamic collaborative task migration technology | Journal on Wireless Communications and Networking | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13638-026-02623-8)\nciteturn9search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last month; # A service cache-based dynamic collaborative task migration technology ... Section 7 concludes with future directions for vehicular edge computing applications. ... Based on the mobile edge computing system model presented in this paper, the methodology for optimizing task migration is established through a joint strategy of dynamic clustering and service pre-caching. ... For MUs, the original server is required to cache their services, whereas for high-traffic users, caching their services by the original server is not mandatory.\n\n# A service cache-based dynamic collaborative task migration technology\n\n  * Research\n  * Open access\n  * Published: 11 May 2026\n\n  * Volume 2026, article number 85 (2026)\n  * Cite this article\n\nYou have full access to this open access article\n\nDownload PDF\n\nSave article\n\nView saved research\n\nJournal on Wireless Communications and Networking Aims and scope Submit manuscript\n\nA service cache-based dynamic collaborative task migration technology\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Xiaoyan Zhao^{1},\n  * Xiao Dong  ORCID: orcid.org/0009-0009-6598-7145^{1},\n  * Yongpan Ren^{1},\n  * Junna Zhang^{1} &\n  * …\n  * Zhitao Gao^{2} \nShow authors\n\n  * 162 Accesses\n\n  * Explore all metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe continuous emergence of compute-intensive and delay-sensitive services in edge networks has promoted the rapid development of task migration technology. ## 2 Methods/experimental\n\nBased on the mobile edge computing system model presented in this paper, the methodology for optimizing task migration is established through a joint strategy of dynamic clustering and service pre-caching. Specifically, this study considers a network environment comprising users, base stations, and edge servers, where migration decisions are driven by user mobility trajectories and server load states. To ensure service continuity and minimize migration costs, the research methodology involves deriving the migration prediction radius and transforming the cost minimization problem into two sub-problems: optimal collaborative clustering and service pre-caching. The DMC algorithm is developed to construct user-centric clusters, while the C-DDQN is proposed to optimize caching decisions by learning from service request patterns.\n\nThe experimental validation is conducted using a real-world desensitized OTT dataset, collecting 47,524 data points from users within a specific geographic area to simulate realistic mobility and service request distributions. The data are categorized into busy- and idle-time periods to rigorously test the system’s robustness under different load conditions. The validity of the proposed algorithms is evaluated by comparing key performance metrics, specifically average migration cost, service latency, and cache hit ratio, against traditional benchmark strategies. The results are analyzed to demonstrate the superiority of the proposed joint optimization framework in reducing delay and improving migration success rates in mobile-aware scenarios.\n\n## 3 Problem formulation\n\n### 3.1 System model\n\nTo depict the scenario of task migration, this study assumes that users, base stations (cells), and edge servers (ESs) in the network are denoted as \\(\\textbf{N}=\\left\\{ 1,2,\\ldots ,N \\right\\}\\), \\(\\textbf{M}=\\left\\{ 1,2,\\ldots ,M \\right\\}\\) and \\(\\textbf{E}=\\left\\{ 1,2,\\ldots ,E \\right\\}\\), respectively. It is noteworthy that not all base stations deploy edge server due to cost constraints, and the actual number and placement are configured based on specific requirements, i.e., \\(E < M\\) .\n### Definition 1\n\n(Mobile user to be migrated (MU)): In the context of mobile awareness, the value of \\(t_{n}^{l}\\) is estimated when the user’s velocity vector forms an angle of \\(\\alpha \\in \\left[ 0,{\\pi }/{2}\\; \\right)\\) concerning the direction of the next cell. If the remaining completion time \\(t_{n}^{end1}\\) for unfinished tasks exceeds the predicted remaining departure time \\(t_{n}^{l}\\), i.e., \\(t_{n}^{end1}>t_{n}^{l}\\), user n will be identified as a mobile user to be migrated.\n\n### Definition 2\n\n(Overloaded user to be migrated (LU)): In the context of mobile awareness, if a new task is generated and the time required for its completion \\(t_{n}^{end2}\\) exceeds the predicted remaining departure time \\(t_{n}^{l}\\), i.e., \\(t_{n}^{end2}>t_{n}^{l}\\), or if the server currently serving is overloaded, user n will be identified as a candidate for relocation and referred to as an overloaded user to be migrated.\n\nFor MUs, the original server is required to cache their services, whereas for high-traffic users, caching their services by the original server is not mandatory. This indicates that the algorithm demonstrates superior performance during peak periods, specifically in scenarios with high service request intensity.\n\nFigure 12 shows the variation of average delay with the number of servers at busy time and idle time. In busy time and idle time, the average delay of the proposed algorithm decreases smoothly with the increase of the number of servers, and the delay is reduced by at least 12.55% compared with other algorithms\n\nFigure 13 illustrates the variation in migration success rates during busy and idle times as a function of the number of servers. For the proposed algorithm, the cluster radius is set to 5. Compared to other algorithms, the proposed method demonstrates a consistently higher success rate during busy periods. During idle periods, the migration success rate approaches that of the Always Migrate strategy, which indicates that the proposed algorithm achieves superior performance specifically during high-traffic phases.\n\nIn summary, the experimental results demonstrate that the proposed joint optimization strategy significantly outperforms traditional methods, achieving a substantial reduction in both migration costs and service delays. These findings confirm that coupling clustering decisions with service pre-caching effectively mitigates the service initialization latency, offering a substantial advantage over reactive approaches for delay-sensitive applications. However, it is worth noting that this study is primarily based on a low-speed mobility model derived from pedestrian data. The current framework assumes relatively stable trajectory predictions, and its robustness in high-speed scenarios, such as Vehicular Edge Computing (IoV) with drastic topology changes, requires further validation. This limitation highlights the necessity of extending our algorithm to handle higher mobility and prediction uncertainties in future work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Performance Optimization Strategies for Distributed Cloud Databases (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/394472742_Performance_Optimization_Strategies_for_Distributed_Cloud_Databases)\nciteturn9search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; level, dataset size and working set proportions are specified to test both cache-resident and disk- ... distributed database engine configured to support tunable replication policies and consistency ... tuning, resource-aware autoscaling with predictive warm-up, and fine-grained throttling to control\n\nand mixed OLTP/OLAP hybrid workloads.\n\nWorkload Characterization\n\nWorkloads are modeled at multiple granularity levels. At the request level, distributions capture\n\nrequest arrival rates, read/write ratios, key access skewness, and transaction lengths. At the data\n\nlevel, dataset size and working set proportions are specified to test both cache-resident and disk-\n\nbound behaviors. Hotspot patterns are introduced using Zipfian distributions to emulate skewed\n\naccess that commonly occurs in production applications. Temporal variations are included to\n\nassess the responsiveness of adaptive strategies under shifting load patterns.\n\nEmulated Cloud Environment\n\nThe emulation platform configures multiple zones representing cloud regions, each with\n\ncontrollable network latency and bandwidth constraints. Nodes in each region run instances of a\n\ndistributed database engine configured to support tunable replication policies and consistency\n\nle vels. The platform supports dynamic reconfiguration of replication, automated failure injection,\n\nand traffic shaping to simulate transient network partitions and node failures.\n\nOptimization Strategies Tested\n\nA set of optimization strategies was implemented and evaluated. These include latency-aware\n\nquery routing, adaptive replication placement, hybrid consistency models with per-operation\n\ntuning, resource-aware autoscaling with predictive warm-up, and fine-grained throttling to control\n\ncontention. Each strategy was evaluated both in isolation and in combination with others to\n\nunderstand interaction effects.\n\nMeasurement and Metrics\n\nPerformance is assessed using multiple metrics. Latency metrics include median latency and tail\n\nlatency percentiles up to p99.9 to capture worst-case user experience. Throughput is measured in\n\noperations per second. Consistency overhead is evaluated by measuring staleness windows for\n\neventual-consistency reads and operation latency under stronger consistency levels. Cost is\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResource Usage Control In Multi-Tenant (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=258b42c242e637775576dc8ee9f1f12239c2485a&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; conﬁguration change) and less disruptive/abrupt (e.g., cachewarm up at start or increasing database size) compared to ... In s